{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli in the Twelfth Century he was born in 1140 and died in 1187. He was the eldest son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. In 1152 Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli.\n\nBiography\n\nIn 1157 Raymond III participated in the Siege of Shaizar. The following year he was made the regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem by his cousin Baldwin III. Raymond III served as regent until 1161 when Baldwin III reached the age of majority. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Harim. He was held captive for eight years until he was ransomed in 1172.\n\nUpon his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and took up the rule of his county once again. In 1174 Raymond III supported the election of Baldwin IV as King of Jerusalem, despite the fact that Baldwin was a leper. In 1177 Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Montgisard, where the Crusader forces defeated Saladin. Raymond III's relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem was strained after the election of Sibylla as Queen in 1186. Raymond III opposed Sibylla's marriage to Guy of Lusignan, who he saw as incompetent.\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III refused to take part in the Battle of Hattin, which resulted in a disastrous defeat for the Crusader forces. Raymond III died later that same year during the Siege of Acre.\n\nFamily\n\nRaymond III was married twice. His first wife was Eschiva of Saint Omer, with whom he had one daughter, Melisende. After Eschiva's death, Raymond III married Eschiva of Ibelin. With Eschiva of Ibelin, Raymond III had two sons, Bohemond and Raymond.\n\nLegacy\n\nRaymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli was marked by a series of military conflicts with the Muslim states surrounding the County of Tripoli. Despite these challenges, Raymond III was able to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli throughout his reign. Raymond III's support for the election of Baldwin IV as King of Jerusalem and his opposition to the marriage of Sibylla and Guy of Lusignan have been seen as attempts to maintain the stability of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Highlighted Section Part 1: Military Conflicts with Muslim States*\n\nDuring Raymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli, the County of Tripoli was surrounded by Muslim states, including the Zengid dynasty and the Ayyubid dynasty. These states posed a significant threat to the County of Tripoli, and Raymond III was frequently engaged in military conflicts with them.\n\n*Highlighted Section Part 2: Support for Baldwin IV*\n\nRaymond III's support for the election of Baldwin IV as King of Jerusalem was an important moment in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Baldwin IV was a leper, and his election was not universally popular. Raymond III's support for Baldwin IV helped to ensure his election and to maintain the stability of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*Highlighted Section Part 3: Opposition to Sibylla and Guy of Lusignan*\n\nRaymond III's opposition to the marriage of Sibylla and Guy of Lusignan has been seen as an attempt to maintain the stability of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III saw Guy of Lusignan as incompetent and feared that his election as King of Jerusalem would lead to instability in the Kingdom. Raymond III's opposition to the marriage was unsuccessful, and Guy of Lusignan was elected King of Jerusalem in 1186.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.2544739246368408}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -2.4676019165781327e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.13004565238952637}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.17482119798660278}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.14697062969207764}, {"token": "o